2. Networking Troubleshooting

What happens when containers use different networks?

Containers connected to separate user-defined bridge networks cannot automatically communicate using each other's container names.

Hands-on Practice

Step 1: Create two separate networks

docker network create day12-net-a
docker network create day12-net-b

Step 2: Run containers on different networks

docker run -d --name web1 --network day12-net-a nginx

docker run -d --name web2 --network day12-net-b nginx

Step 3: Test communication

docker exec -it web1 bash

curl http://web2

Error received:

Could not resolve host: web2

Step 4: Fix the problem

docker network connect day12-net-a web2

Tested communication again and successfully received the Nginx response.

What I learned: A container can connect to multiple networks. Connecting "web2" to "day12-net-a" allowed both containers to share a network and resolve each other's names.

