4. Practical Project – Nginx Website Using Compose

Step 1: Create the project structure

day12-compose/
├── compose.yaml
└── website/
    └── index.html

Created a custom HTML website inside "website/index.html".

Step 2: Create the Compose file

services:
  website:
    image: nginx:latest
    container_name: day12-website
    ports:
      - "8080:80"
    volumes:
      - ./website:/usr/share/nginx/html:ro

Understanding the Configuration

Configuration| What it does
"services"| Defines the application services.
"website"| Identifies the Compose service.
"image"| Specifies the image used to create the container.
"container_name"| Gives the container a custom name.
"ports"| Connects host and container ports.
"volumes"| Mounts host files or directories inside the container.

New Concept: YAML

YAML is a human-readable format used to store configuration settings.

Docker Compose uses YAML so that service settings can be written in a structured way. Indentation matters because it defines which settings belong to which service.

New Concept: Port Mapping

ports:
  - "8080:80"

The format is "HOST_PORT:CONTAINER_PORT".

- "8080" is the port on my host machine.
- "80" is the port where Nginx listens inside the container.

When I open "localhost:8080", Docker forwards the traffic to port 80 inside the container.

New Concept: Bind Mount

volumes:
  - ./website:/usr/share/nginx/html:ro

A bind mount connects a specific directory on the host machine to a directory inside a container.

In my project:

- "./website" is the host directory.
- "/usr/share/nginx/html" is Nginx's website directory.
- "ro" means read-only from the container's perspective.

This is useful during development because I can edit website files on my machine and see the changes without rebuilding the Docker image.

